NodeIds":["cnm_h185"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h185_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_021","cnm_075"],"id":"semiaxis_focal_relation","label":"Use the ellipse parameter relation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"semiaxis_focal_relation":["cnm_h185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h185","cnm_021","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:147263846818a7876edf5496"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"24","reason":"c=√(13²-5²)=√144=12, so the distance between the foci is 2c=24.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h185"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h185_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_021","cnm_075"],"id":"compute_focal_separation","label":"Find c and double it","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compute_focal_separation":["cnm_h185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h185","cnm_021","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:bc14829b3a824b71f834c566"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"5","reason":"c²=3²+4²=25, so c=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h190"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h190_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_020","cnm_075"],"id":"main","label":"Use the hyperbola parameter relation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"main":["cnm_h190"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h190","cnm_020","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:147263846818a7876edf5496","alignment:a9983c39a03c3ae9f7b08807"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 8√1003","reason":"Let the rectangle sides be u and v, so uv = 2006 and 2a = u+v. Since c² = (u²+v²)/4, b² = a²-c² = uv/2 = 1003. The ellipse area gives ab = 2006, hence a = 2√1003 and the rectangle perimeter is 4a = 8√1003.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h185","cnm_h183"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_p089","cnm_p078"],"id":"focal-sum-and-semiaxes","label":"Relate the rectangle sides to the ellipse axes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"focal-sum-and-semiaxes":["cnm_h183","cnm_h185","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h183","cnm_h185","cnm_174","cnm_186","cnm_p089","cnm_p078"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:147263846818a7876edf5496","alignment:23cabb15eddf9533bafee530"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (7)","reason":"The roots give points (1,0), (-1,±sqrt(3)), and (-2,±sqrt(2)). Their ellipse is 5x^2+6y^2+9x-14=0, or 5(x+9/10)^2+6y^2=361/20. Thus a^2=361/100, b^2=361/120, and eccentricity squared is 1-b^2/a^2=1/6. Therefore m+n=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h185","cnm_h184"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"symmetric-ellipse-fitting","label":"Fit an ellipse using symmetry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-ellipse-fitting":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_136","cnm_h184","cnm_090","cnm_h185"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_136","cnm_h184","cnm_090","cnm_h185"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:147263846818a7876edf5496","alignment:23cabb15eddf9533bafee530","alignment:a9983c39a03c3ae9f7b08807"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, e=22/23","reason":"Let the large and small major semiaxes be A,a, with A>a>0. Equal eccentricity e gives minor/major ratio k=sqrt(1-e^2)>0 in both, so their standard equations show that they are uniformly similar with length ratio A/a. The general similar-plane-area theorem gives A/a=sqrt(2025)=45; no absolute ellipse-area formula is used. The foci order F-G-H puts the center separation at d=e(A+a)>0. To justify the common right vertex without trusting drawing scale, place the large center at 0 and the small at d on the x-axis. A small-boundary point is (d+a*u,k*a*v), with u^2+v^2=1. Substitution in the large ellipse gives normalized squared level(d^2+a^2+2*d*a*u)/A^2, whose maximum is (d+a)^2/A^2 at u=1. Containment and contact therefore force d+a=A; otherwise the smaller ellipse would either protrude or be strictly interior. Hence e(A+a)=A-a and e=(45-1)/(45+1)=22/23, D.","recordedAnswer":"D","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h185","cnm_h184","cnm_h183"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_054","cnm_042","cnm_p088","cnm_p185"],"id":"focal-sums-and-area-scaling","label":"Focal distance sums and area scaling","methodDirectNodeIds":{"focal-sums-and-area-scaling":["cnm_h183","cnm_h185","cnm_h186","math:similar-figure-area-scaling"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h183","cnm_h185","cnm_h186","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_054","cnm_042","cnm_p088","cnm_p185"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"similar-ellipses-and-internal-contact","label":"Use equal-eccentricity similarity and prove the axis contact","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-ellipses-and-internal-contact":["math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_h184","cnm_h186","cnm_072","cnm_h018","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_h184","cnm_h186","cnm_072","cnm_h018","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:a9983c39a03c3ae9f7b08807"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"10","reason":"The sum of the distances to the foci is 2a=2×5=10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h183"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h183_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h183"],"id":"m1","label":"Use the focal-distance sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h187"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h187","cnm_h183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:23cabb15eddf9533bafee530"